Or maybe then afterwards
I went back to my desk

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:43.030
and then continued working,

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:45.430
but getting the little exercise unit in,

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:47.660
that was always possible
from my experience.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:49.596
- Yeah, yeah definitely.

00:12:49.596 --> 00:12:52.920
So that's a good routine
that you established,

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:55.690
always having three workouts a week.

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:58.190
I mean, if you have one day in a week

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:03.090
in your four days of project
work, and everybody knows,

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:05.070
especially your boss knows,

00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:09.780
then that's a reason that
the boss will leave you alone

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:10.613
at that time.

00:13:10.613 --> 00:13:15.613
So if the boss knows, okay
Heinrich goes running at 7:00 pm

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:18.810
and he always goes running
at 7:00 pm on a Monday,

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:21.270
he knows that's your holy time.

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:24.368
And everyone, also your
colleagues will leave you alone.

00:13:24.368 --> 00:13:26.620
- And I think, I completely agree Marie.

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:29.470
How I always did it, especially
when I was an Associate

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:30.970
in one side and was the Project Leader,

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:33.296
of course you maybe will
have a bit more flexibility

00:13:33.296 --> 00:13:34.510
to organize your day,

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:37.030
but in the beginning of the project,

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:39.470
I always made sure to
have such a conversation

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:41.400
with my Project Lead, with my Team Lead,

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:42.970
that kind of a two-sided conversation,

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:45.200
what was really important to me in terms

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:47.150
of the way of working and
handling all these things,

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:48.990
and what was important
to the Project Lead.

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:50.700
And at least at McKinsey,
there was a bit of

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:53.090
kind of structure, just a standardized way

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:54.390
of having these conversations, right,

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:55.850
so this was quite typical.

00:13:55.850 --> 00:13:58.380
But then I always made it
clear that it was just very

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:01.760
important for me to get a
proper unit of longer exercise

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:04.400
in once every week from
Monday to Thursday,

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:06.400
and I always try to pick one day

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:08.640
to make that stick and
to make everybody know.

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:11.310
And then from my experience,
even though of course

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:12.650
the characters of Project Leads

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:15.150
and so on can be quite different, right,

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:18.200
but from my experience there
was always the possibility

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:20.030
to kind of discuss this and agree

00:14:20.030 --> 00:14:22.964
with the Project Lead of
that, and from my experience

00:14:22.964 --> 00:14:24.440
it's just important just to be explicit.

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:27.280
Just make it really clear
that this is your expectations

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:28.840
as Associate to be able to do that,

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:32.110
and usually you will find
that people will agree.

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:36.280
There should be an opportunity
once a week to carve an hour

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:38.730
of sports out of your evening
or of your morning, right?

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:41.020
So people will agree and
this will be possible,

00:14:41.020 --> 00:14:43.180
but it's also a matter
of you demanding that,

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:46.543
of you making it clear that
this is really important to you.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:48.820
Just my two cents on that.

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:49.963
- Yeah, great.

00:14:49.963 --> 00:14:52.910
- No, good, so that's exercise.

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:56.640
So let's now jump to the
third topic, and this is food.

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:57.900
And I know this is a topic

00:14:57.900 --> 00:14:59.460
you're especially passionate about.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:00.293
- Exactly.

00:15:00.293 --> 00:15:03.230
So in my book I promote the vegan,

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:06.590
gluten free, and no sugar lifestyle.

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:11.580
I know this sounds very strange
for maybe a lot of you guys.

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:16.580
And I don't recommend every
one of you to cut everything

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:19.620
from one day to the other.

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:22.727
I know Heinrich, you love your
steak and you love your meat.

00:15:22.727 --> 00:15:23.890
- I do.

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:28.890
- But what I would recommend
for everyone who has some time.

00:15:29.417 --> 00:15:32.630
You don't really need a
lot of time to eat healthy.

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:36.650
Is maybe start with cutting the sugar.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:40.130
I know especially on a very long workday,

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:43.490
those chocolate bars
are very tempting, yeah?

00:15:43.490 --> 00:15:47.310
And obviously the longer you
work, the better they taste.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:50.020
- So let's talk about a
typical day of somebody working

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:51.880
in a law firm or in a consulting firm

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:53.340
or a busy corporate job.

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:55.140
What would you recommend them to eat

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:57.440
at the different times of the day?

00:15:57.440 --> 00:15:59.940
- So I would have three big meals.

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:02.880
I would start with
breakfast, which is oatmeal

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:05.550
and keeps you full for a couple of hours.

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:07.900
Then I would eat a light lunch,

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:10.330
maybe some salad with some proteins.

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:13.990
And maybe something warm in the evening,

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:16.160
if you do have the time
to eat something warm.

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:19.120
And as a snack, I would
include two snacks.

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:22.600
I would include some nuts
and some dried fruit.

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:23.950
- Why nuts?

00:16:23.950 --> 00:16:26.310
- They're especially good
for your brain health.

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:28.860
So I would recommend eating walnuts,

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:31.658
if you're not allergic to them obviously.

00:16:31.658 --> 00:16:33.858
They're very, very good
for your brain health.

00:16:33.858 --> 00:16:34.691
- Good.

00:16:34.691 --> 00:16:36.030
And just to second that, right,

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:39.060
so especially these
snacks in the team room.

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:40.980
I mean this is such a trap, right?

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:43.210
There are so many experiences
that I had in team room

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:45.550
where people just
started to buy chocolate,

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:48.350
just leave it there for
everybody just to eat.

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:52.310
And this is just so difficult
then to resist the temptation.

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:54.640
And from my experience, in the end,

00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:58.110
what it always came down to,
if you want to change that,

00:16:58.110 --> 00:17:00.440
was always up to one
person in the team then

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:02.620
just to stand up and say hey guys,

00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:05.530
let's not have these
chocolate snacks all the time.

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:07.350
But let's get some other snacks.

00:17:07.350 --> 00:17:09.650
And just somebody taking the initiative

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:13.430
and then organizing some other
stuff from the supermarket

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:15.710
around next corner or whatever
your opportunities are

00:17:15.710 --> 00:17:18.610
from wherever your workplace
is or wherever the client was.

00:17:18.610 --> 00:17:20.840
So why shouldn't that be you right?

00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:23.320
So just take the initiative
if you believe that your team

00:17:23.320 --> 00:17:25.890
is developing really unhealthy
habits which then, of course,

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:29.060
often leads also to
yourself eating unhealthy.

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:31.330
Take the initiative yourself
and just make an impact.

00:17:31.330 --> 00:17:34.020
Say that you want to change
that and then trust me,

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:37.630
everybody on the team
will be grateful for that.

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:38.550
- Yeah, exactly.

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:41.820
So what I promote in my book
is a vegan, gluten free,

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:44.370
and no added sugar lifestyle.

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:47.830
And obviously those chocolate
bars do count to sugar

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:51.730
so just maybe standup
and tell your teammates

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:54.150
no chocolate bars any longer.

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:57.620
And if it's some bars then
maybe it's the very healthy

00:17:57.620 --> 00:18:00.040
nut bars you can buy in
the supermarket as well.

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:01.500
- This is also one of my experiences.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:03.897
I just remember one team I had,

00:18:03.897 --> 00:18:07.380
we developed this habit of
pretty much once a week or so

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:12.380
we just bought from the
supermarket a huge box of nut bars.

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:15.220
And maybe then they also had
a little bit of chocolate

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:17.760
on there but not like
a full chocolate bar.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:22.243
But, probably this wasn't the
most healthy snack out there.

00:18:22.243 --> 00:18:25.920
But it was much better
than these huge bars

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:28.480
of chocolate or candy that
you just unfortunately have

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:30.640
so often in these team rooms.

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:31.473
- But be careful.

00:18:31.473 --> 00:18:34.950
I know it's a good thing
to start with nut bars.

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:37.890
There are a lot of nut bars,
especially in supermarkets,

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:39.210
that include a lot of sugar.

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:41.180
Maybe just turn the nut bar around

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:43.120
and then look for added sugar.

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:46.200
That's the thing you always
want to look out, added sugar.

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:49.340
If it's naturally included,
it's not that bad for you.

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:51.850
- Yeah, we don't want added sugar.

00:18:51.850 --> 00:18:55.550
Okay, that's some great
insights into the food.

00:18:55.550 --> 00:18:58.550
Let's now come to sleep.

00:18:58.550 --> 00:19:02.120
I mean, and again, this is
just such a difficult topic,

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:06.010
you're just chronically,
constantly sleeping not enough

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:08.180
unfortunately you're on so many projects.

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:10.670
Do we have any suggestions what you can do

00:19:10.670 --> 00:19:13.290
to maybe just improve the
quality of your sleep?

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:15.940
Or just the, still then, nevertheless,

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:17.270
the extent of your sleep?

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:18.740
What are your suggestions on that?

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:22.420
- Okay, so obviously I cannot
change your working hours.

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:24.270
That's something you can't change

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:28.730
and if your partner demands
that slide to be finished maybe

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:32.560
at 2:00 am in the morning,
obviously you cannot say no.

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:36.060
- Well I would not
necessarily agree with that,

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:38.620
I think there are some ways
you can push back also on that,

00:19:38.620 --> 00:19:40.620
but maybe that's a
topic for another video,

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:42.170
but let's just assume
you cannot change it.

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:46.900
- Okay, so obviously
then a lot of you guys

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:49.300
take out their phone when
they're already in bed

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:51.770
and then they start checking messages.

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:54.120
This is something I would cut.

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:56.890
It's proven that if you check your phone

00:19:56.890 --> 00:20:01.130
and also check a screen directly
before you start sleeping,

00:20:01.130 --> 00:20:06.130
your sleep is much
worse than if you don't.

00:20:06.310 --> 00:20:10.100
So, if it's possible for
you, try to half an hour

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:14.970
before you go to sleep, not
check any screens any longer, A.

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:19.970
And B, try to meditate for
five minutes before you sleep.

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:22.560
Sit down in your hotel
room, close your eyes,

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:25.360
and then do a body scan.

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:29.200
And when you sleep, what
is also really healthy

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:33.550
and what promotes good sleep is lavender,

00:20:33.550 --> 00:20:38.500
maybe a lavender oil, you
can rub on your temples.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:42.348
And lavender is proven
to promote good sleep.

00:20:42.348 --> 00:20:43.710
- Okay, I've never tried that

00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:46.310
but maybe we'll try that in the future.

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:47.143
Good.

00:20:47.143 --> 00:20:50.610
Just two things also just
adding from my experience

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:54.250
working in consulting, what
I felt was really helpful,

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:56.240
two tips, so first,

00:20:56.240 --> 00:20:59.240
in the early years of
working in consulting,

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:02.910
I just drank so much coffee all day,

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:04.870
even until late in the evening,

00:21:04.870 --> 00:21:08.680
and I just was convinced that
it didn't really effect me

00:21:08.680 --> 00:21:10.980
in the sense that I
was still able to sleep

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:12.990
even though I just drank several cups

00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:14.710
of coffee or anything like that.

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:18.300
What I then realized over time
is that, while it was true,

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:21.620
while it was correct that
I was still able to sleep,

00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:24.380
somehow I realized that
the quality of my sleep

00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:25.570
lacked a bit, right?

00:21:25.570 --> 00:21:27.990
And then I did a bit of
research and I found out that

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:31.470
the half period time of caffeine can take

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:33.240
up to four to five hours,

00:21:33.240 --> 00:21:35.010
and I found that quite insane, right?

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:40.010
So this means if you have
a cup of coffee at 6:00 pm

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:42.620
in the evening, that means
that then around midnight,

00:21:42.620 --> 00:21:46.780
you still have half of
the caffeine in your body.

00:21:46.780 --> 00:21:48.620
And that really made me thinking,

00:21:48.620 --> 00:21:51.820
that made me realize
that probably I shouldn't

00:21:51.820 --> 00:21:54.300
drink coffee or take in
other sources of caffeine

00:21:55.630 --> 00:21:59.780
in the afternoon, so I usually
try after 3:00 pm, 4:00 pm,

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:02.180
not drink any coffee anymore.

00:22:02.180 --> 00:22:05.600
And at least subjectively, I
felt that my sleep improved.

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:08.233
And the second tip that
I develop over time,

00:22:08.233 --> 00:22:10.830
I found out over time,
that for me it really helps

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:15.130
to put the smartphone on
airplane mode while I sleep.

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:18.810
And not sure why exactly, I'm
not sure if this is because

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:21.800
all the frequencies that
maybe don't happen anymore,

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:24.370
I'm not sure if there's any
scientific rationale for that

00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:25.961
or maybe it's just knowing

00:22:25.961 --> 00:22:27.130
that the phone will not disturb you,

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:29.540
that nobody will call you at
night or anything like that.

00:22:29.540 --> 00:22:32.430
But just having the phone on
airplane mode, at least for me

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:35.630
it really improved my
sleep quality over time.

00:22:35.630 --> 00:22:36.780
- Great idea, Heinrich.

00:22:38.427 --> 00:22:39.260
- Good.

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:41.420
So anything you'd like to add on that?

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:45.090
- No I think that's one
section obviously as well.

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:47.170
Try to reduce your coffee intake,

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:51.360
rather drink some green tea
instead of the caffeine.

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:55.660
But I think everyone can
handle that the way they like.

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:56.493
- Yeah perfect.

00:22:56.493 --> 00:22:57.961
So, I think this now has been

00:22:57.961 --> 00:22:59.308
quite a comprehensive discussion.

00:22:59.308 --> 00:23:01.491
So we talked about morning routines,

00:23:01.491 --> 00:23:05.600
we talked about exercising,
about food, about sleep.

00:23:05.600 --> 00:23:08.350
So thank you very much, Marie.

00:23:08.350 --> 00:23:10.270
But now also I'd like to
give you this opportunity

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:12.420
just to introduce your new book a bit.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:14.050
What is it, tell us about it.

00:23:14.050 --> 00:23:15.760
- Thanks, so this is my new book.

00:23:15.760 --> 00:23:19.870
You can buy it on Amazon as a
print on demand or an eBook.

00:23:19.870 --> 00:23:24.130
So if you're on your job,
then you can maybe download it

00:23:24.130 --> 00:23:26.850
on your Kindle or maybe your iPhone.

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:30.510
And yeah, it does include a lot of recipes

00:23:30.510 --> 00:23:35.440
that are very, very healthy
such as some oatmeal,

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:40.440
some almond mousse, or some porridge.

00:23:40.660 --> 00:23:43.530
And then maybe how to start your day.

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:47.800
Here you see the lemon water,
as I already discussed.

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:50.270
And obviously book two
is already in the making,

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:54.720
so I hope you will buy my
book and I hope you like it.

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:56.980
- I can completely second
that and it's a great book.

00:23:56.980 --> 00:23:59.400
I read it myself and of
course will leave a link

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:02.930
to the book below in the video
description, so check it out.

00:24:02.930 --> 00:24:04.620
And thank you, Marie!

00:24:04.620 --> 00:24:06.210
That's it, I really appreciate

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:07.560
to hear this interview with you.

00:24:07.560 --> 00:24:09.940
I think there were some
really great and tangible tips

00:24:09.940 --> 00:24:11.360
that we all can profit from.

